      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the median of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\) and find its mean, we start by analyzing the set and identifying its median. The set contains five integers, and the median is the middle value when the set is ordered. Since the number is 5, the middle value is the third term.\n\nThe set can be ordered as \\(\\{n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\}\\). The third term in this ordered set is \\(n+6\\). According to the problem, this term is equal to 9. Thus, we have:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 9.\n\\]\nSolving for \\(n\\), we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\nn = 9 - 6 = 3.\n\\]\nWith \\(n\\) determined, the set now becomes \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the mean of these five numbers. The mean is the sum of the numbers divided by the count of the numbers. The sum of the numbers is:\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50.\n\\]\nSince the set contains five numbers, the mean is:\n\\[\n\\frac{50}{5} = 10.\n\\]\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
