      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the median of the set $\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}$, we first sort the set in increasing order. If we denote the sorted set as $\\{d_1, d_2, d_3, d_4, d_5\\}$, then $d_1 = n$, $d_2 = n + 5$, $d_3 = n + 6$, $d_4 = n + 9$, and $d_5 = n + 15$. \nSince the median is the middle number of the set, which in this case is $n + 6$, we have $n+6=9$. Solving for $n$, we find that $n=3$. \nNow, we substitute the value of $n$ into the set to get $\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}$. To find the mean, we add up all the numbers in the set and divide by 5, which is the number of elements in the set: $$\\frac{3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18}{5} = \\boxed{10}.$$",
